# Line-ending policy
#
# The corpus anchors ratified texts by SHA-256 over file content, and gnomon-ledger will
# build a hash chain on the same bytes. Without a policy, `core.autocrlf` decides per
# machine: the same file hashes differently on Windows and Linux, and a chain computed on
# one platform fails verification on the other.
#
# Before this file existed the repository had already drifted — 2 of 70 text files were
# stored with CRLF and 68 with LF, by accident of how they were uploaded. See LEDGER.md
# and the anchor supersession recorded in the two corpus ledgers.
#
# `eol=lf` is deliberate and does more than pick a convention: in a FRESH CHECKOUT it makes
# the working tree byte-identical to the stored blob, so `sha256sum <file>` equals
# `git cat-file blob <rev>:<file> | sha256sum`.
#
# It does NOT retroactively rewrite an existing working tree — git leaves already-present
# files alone — so a tree that predates this policy can still hold CRLF while the index
# holds LF. The anchors therefore continue to name the committed blob as canonical. Verified
# both ways: a fresh clone reports `i/lf w/lf`, this repository's pre-existing tree reports
# `i/lf w/crlf`. Refresh a stale tree with `git rm --cached -r . && git reset --hard`.
#
# Check what git actually stored with `git ls-files --eol`, never by grepping for a carriage
# return — passing a bare CR as a shell argument silently yields an empty pattern that
# matches every line. See .github/workflows/fixtures.yml.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the formats the services read, so a future `text=auto` heuristic miss
# cannot silently reintroduce the drift.
*.md         text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.json       text eol=lf
*.lock       text eol=lf
*.sh         text eol=lf
LICENSE*     text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Binary — never normalize, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
